---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const vanilla = `import { bindHotkey } from "@skryensya/vanilla/hotkey";

// 'mod' is ⌘ on Mac and Ctrl on the rest, a single spec, the
// dos plataformas.
const off = bindHotkey("mod+k", () => palette.showModal());

// returns its own cleanliness, like every enhancer
off();`;

const react = `import { useHotkey } from "@skryensya/react/hotkey";

function Palette() {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  useHotkey("mod+k", () => setOpen((v) => !v));
  // …
}`;

const specs = `"mod+k"        // ⌘K on Mac, Ctrl+K on the rest
"mod+shift+p"  // exact combination: DOES NOT fire with "mod+p"
"shift+/"      // "?" for help
"escape"       // a single key, no modifier `;

const format = `import { formatHotkey } from "@skryensya/core/hotkey";

formatHotkey("mod+k", true);   // "⌘K"   (Mac)
formatHotkey("mod+k", false);  // "Ctrl+K"
// the badge next to the search engine above is filled so`;
---

<Base title="Hotkey" description="The keyboard shortcut primitive: a framework-agnostic matcher, an imperative bindHotkey, and a React useHotkey.">
  <h1>Hotkey</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A keyboard shortcut is behavior with no equivalent on the platform, <code>accesskey</code>
    It exists but the browser hides it behind its own chords and never gives a ⌘K type trigger. So
    that the system sends it, matched like all behavior: the pure matcher in
    <code>@skryensya/core</code>, the imperative binding in <code>@skryensya/vanilla</code>, the hook
    declarative in <code>@skryensya/react</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Try it now: press <kbd class="sk-kbd" data-sk-command-palette-hint>⌘K</kbd>, the search engine for this documentation
    is <a href="/en/components/command-palette">CommandPalette</a>, the component that consumes this primitive. The <a href="/en/components/kbd"><code>Kbd</code></a> has
    also a state that reacts to the keyboard with this same <code>matchesHotkey</code>; seen in his
    page.
  </p>

  <h2>Why <code>mod</code> is worth it</h2>
  <p>
    That is why this deserves a primitive and not a loose <code>event.metaKey</code>:
    <code>mod</code> is ⌘ on a Mac and Ctrl on everything else, so a single spec, 
    <code>"mod+k"</code>, is the correct chord in both without the writer ever branching out
    platform. The core takes that data as a boolean instead of sniffing at <code>navigator</code>, like this
    that remains pure and testable; The bindings detect it and pass it on.
  </p>

  <h2>Vanilla</h2>
  <p>
    <code>bindHotkey(spec, handler)</code> hooks a listener from <code>keydown</code> and returns its
    own cleaning, the same way that returns every system enhancer.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={vanilla} label="JavaScript" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>
    <code>useHotkey(spec, handler)</code> is the same as hook: it consumes the same core matcher
    (never the vanilla package, the two bindings are siblings on the core), pass the handler through a ref
    so as not to re-hook on each render, and clearing the effect removes the listener.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={react} label="React" />

  <h2>The spec</h2>
  <p>
    Modifiers and a key, separated by <code>+</code>, in any order. The chord is
    <strong>exact</strong>: <code>"mod+k"</code> does not fire with <code>mod+shift+k</code>, so a
    shortcut do not silently swallow a superset that the app wanted for something else.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={specs} label="specs" lang="ts" />

  <h2>Mostrarlo</h2>
  <p>
    A shortcut is only discovered if it is seen. <code>formatHotkey</code> writes it as the system writes it
    operational, glyphs on Mac, words on the rest, for the badge next to a search engine or in a menu.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={format} label="JavaScript" />

  <h2>What it does for you, and what it does not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Does not fire a single letter as you type.</strong> A <code>"/in"</code> inside a
      input is text, not a command, it is suppressed. But a chord with a modifier (⌘K) <em>always</em>
      shoot, because it has to reach you from inside the search engine that opens. It can be forced with
      <code>enableWhileTyping</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stops the default browser.</strong> A ⌘S that also saves the page is a bug, so
      which calls <code>preventDefault()</code> by default.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Does not handle sequences</strong> (type <code>g</code> after <code>g</code>) or scopes
      widget: is a chord and a handler. The rest is put together by whoever consumes it, like the search engine above.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Base>
